    GH-1282: "8051: Decompiling the simplest code."
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/1282

    Source (Keil, opt off):
      byte xdata _buf[0x80];
      void Fn1() { byte i; for (i=0; i<0x80; i++) _buf[i] = 0; }

    8051 models the SUBB borrow / CY flag as bit 7 of PSW, so the loop bound
    `i < 0x80` is lowered to `((i < 0x80) << 7) s>> 7` (boolean shifted into the
    sign position and arithmetically shifted back = a 0/-1 sign-extended mask).
    Upstream's RuleLeftRight cancels `(V << c) s>> c` only when c is a multiple
    of 8, so for the boolean case (c==7) the shift-by-7 carry noise survives all
    the way into the rendered C:

      while (uVar1 = 0x80 - (((uVar2 < 0x80) << 7) >> 7), 0x80 < uVar1) {

    Stage model: S3 "simplification-quiescence" (which algebraic/peephole rules
    fire).  The kuna fix is a new simplification rule (group "analysis", gated by
    the default-off arch flag fold_boolean_mask) that recognizes
    `(b << k) s>> k` with NZMask(b) fitting in the low (width*8 - k) bits and
    rewrites it to the sign-extended-mask form `0 - b`, eliminating the
    shift-by-7 noise.  Enabled by `option booleanmask on`.

    Pass 1 (default pipeline) reproduces the bug (assert #1 works today).
    Pass 2 (after `option booleanmask on`) shows the shift-by-7 noise is gone
    (assert #2): no `<< 7) >> 7` appears anywhere in the output.

